    "set": [
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "a126ca530c8e48d5b88882c734c38935"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "Reading First program and evaluation context",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "property": {
          "$bytes": "e3e363d1dd294ccb8e6ff3b76d99bc33"
        },
        "value": {
          "type": "text",
          "value": "## Funding and local implementation\n\nReading First supported early reading curricula and materials, teacher professional development and coaching, and screening, intervention and progress monitoring. The five reading components were phonemic awareness, phonics, vocabulary, fluency and comprehension.\n\nFederal guidance established common priorities, while states and districts retained choices about materials, assessments, providers and allocation of resources. This was a funding program, not one identical curriculum delivered at every participating school.\n\n## Relationship to the evaluation\n\nThe national impact study defined treatment as receipt of Reading First funding at the school level. Its funded and comparison schools are evaluation groups, not separate national programs. The linked dataset contains the selected achievement, classroom-practice and implementation results and distinguishes adjusted impacts from actual and estimated counterfactual means.\n\nThe national program's reach differs from the evaluation sample. Matched delivery costs and a complete directory of local operators and implementations are not yet available in this dataset.\n\n## Source\n\n[Gamse and colleagues, Reading First Impact Study Final Report](https://ies.ed.gov/sites/default/files/migrated/nces_pubs/ncee/pdf/20094038.pdf), Executive Summary, printed pp. xvi-xvii (PDF pp. 17-18).",
          "language": {
            "$bytes": "090adac0fca4822e8e719263e67620ec"
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "unset": []
  },
